# Record 586
r=586
talk(r,'1983-02-28','I. M. Krichever','P001086-P001090','Hessians and perturbations of finite gap KdV solutions',r'The actual notes start on P001087 and end above Wodzicki\textquotesingle s heading on P001089. They describe the spectral diagonalization of a stationary KdV Hessian and a basis for first order perturbation theory; Gelfand asks for higher variations. The catalogue range includes the end of Arnold and the start of Wodzicki.')
item(r,'P001087','construction','KdV Hamiltonians, stationary finite gap solutions, and Lenard recursion',r'The notes write the perturbed KdV equation, commuting integrals \(I_k\), \(H=I_{n+2}-\sum c_kI_k\), and \(\delta H/\delta u=0\); the recursion is explicitly qualified as being up to coefficients.',r'For smooth real periodic potentials, the stationary equations of the KdV hierarchy are Euler--Lagrange equations for linear combinations of its local integrals. With suitable normalization they characterize the nonsingular finite gap potentials of genus at most \(n\). The two compatible Hamiltonian operators produce the Lenard recursion. In the displayed convention \(u_t=\partial_x(\tfrac14u_{xx}-\tfrac34u^2)\), a Hamiltonian is \(\int(-\tfrac18u_x^2-\tfrac14u^3)\,dx\).',r'This is background already used in \citet[pp.~1312--1313]{krichever1983hessians}; the paper distinguishes it from its new Hessian theorem.',[K],reconstruction=r'Smooth periodicity, reality, and the Hamiltonian normalization have been supplied; the notebook conflates a density with its integral.',literal='false as written')
item(r,'P001088','construction','Band edges and normalized quasimomentum',r'For \(L=-\partial_x^2+u_0\), the notes draw \(n\) gaps and double points and give \(i\,dp=Q(E)dE/\sqrt{R(E)}\), with vanishing gap periods.',r'For a real smooth periodic finite gap Hill operator with \(n\) open gaps, its desingularized spectral curve is \(y^2=\prod_{j=1}^{2n+1}(E-E_j)\). Its quasimomentum differential is the unique second kind differential with the prescribed principal part at infinity and zero \(a\)-periods. Its numerator has one zero in each open gap. The remaining closed gap points are double eigenvalues of the periodic or antiperiodic problems.',r'The precise differential and periodicity constraints are recalled in \citet[pp.~1313--1314, eq.~(5)]{krichever1983hessians}; this spectral geometry predates the talk.',[K],reconstruction=r'The overall factor, including the printed \(i\), depends on whether the Bloch multiplier is written as \(e^{ipT}\) or \(e^{pT}\); no normalization is silently identified across these conventions.')
item(r,'P001088','theorem','Spectral form of the stationary Hessian',r'Equation (8) gives \(\delta^2H=\tfrac12\sum Q(E_i)(\delta E_i)^2+\tfrac14\sum_sQ(e_s)(\delta e_s)^2\), where \(\delta e_s\) is an opened gap width.',r'At a stationary real finite gap potential, the Hessian of the corresponding hierarchy functional is diagonal in first order old band-edge shifts and quadratic opened-gap coordinates. In Krichever\textquotesingle s normalization it has the form of equation (6) of his paper, reproduced in the notes as equation (8). The shifts are constrained by the fixed-period equations, so the signs of \(Q\) alone cannot count the negative directions in an unconstrained Euclidean space.',kt,[K],cls='A',reconstruction=r'The statement concerns the quadratic term at the stationary potential, not an exact identity for finite perturbations. The overall normalization of the hierarchy integrals must be inherited from the publication; the notebook\textquotesingle s preceding \(I_3\) normalization is inconsistent.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(r,'P001088','claim','First order opening of a closed spectral gap',r'The squared gap width is written as \(|\langle\delta u\,\Psi^2\rangle|^2/\langle|\Psi|^2\rangle\); the constant potential case is identified with Fourier coefficients.',r'Let a double periodic or antiperiodic eigenvalue have an orthonormal basis \(\phi_1,\phi_2\) of its two-dimensional eigenspace. For a real perturbation \(\varepsilon v\), its two first order eigenvalue shifts are the eigenvalues of \(M_{ij}=\langle\phi_i,v\phi_j\rangle\). Thus the leading squared gap width is \(\varepsilon^2[(M_{11}-M_{22})^2+4|M_{12}|^2]\). For the free operator this becomes \(4\varepsilon^2|\widehat v(2k)|^2\) with normalized plane waves.',r'\citet[Lemma~1 and Theorem~2]{krichever1983hessians} gives the spectral perturbation form. \ours{} The invariant two-dimensional compression is our reconstruction, which makes the missing normalization visible.',[K],cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction=r'\ours{} The displayed matrix statement fixes the Hilbert-space normalization explicitly; its eigenvalue separation is checked in independent_checks.py. Rescaling \(\Psi\) changes the notebook\textquotesingle s right side but cannot change the gap, so the formula is not correct without an unstated fixed normalization.')
item(r,'P001088','question','Gelfand on third and higher variations',r'Gelfand asks: \ru{посчитать 3-ю вариацию и т.п.}',r'At a fixed stationary finite gap potential, calculate third and higher variations of the chosen functional \(H\). A third Fr\'echet derivative in potential coordinates is well-defined once \(H\) and the function space are fixed. An analogue of the preceding spectral diagonalization requires additional choices of spectral coordinates and how a double point is unfolded.',r'The located exact paper concerns the Hessian and first order perturbation theory, not a general higher-variation theorem. Searches for the author, Hessians, higher/third variations, and finite gap perturbations did not identify a paper with the unspecified spectral-coordinate conclusion.',[K],cls='F',reconstruction=r'The context suggests a spectral-coordinate formula, but the notes do not say that. We do not turn a request to calculate higher derivatives into an invariant open conjecture.',status='As of November 2026, ordinary higher variations are calculable from the differential-polynomial Hamiltonians; the stronger requested form cannot be identified from these notes.',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',confidence='medium')
item(r,'P001089 (upper portion)','method','Gap-opening modes and their adjoint pairing',r'The notes expand the first order perturbation in \(w_s^\pm\), obtained by opening one additional gap, and append band-edge and divisor derivatives. They give a logarithmic theta expression and an orthogonality formula with mismatched indices.',r'Differentiation of an exact family of KdV solutions gives a solution of the linearized equation. At a finite gap background, gap-opening modes together with tangent vectors to the finite gap family give Krichever\textquotesingle s first order perturbation expansion; squares of the corresponding Bloch solutions solve the adjoint equation and give the projections. The parameter variations must satisfy the fixed-period constraints.',r'\citet[Lemmas~2--3, eqs.~(13)--(19), Theorem~3]{krichever1983hessians} gives the exact basis and forced first order equations. Its mode formula uses a theta quotient with shifts \(\pm2A(e_s)\), differentiated twice, rather than the notebook\textquotesingle s logarithm of a single shifted theta function.',[K],cls='A',reconstruction=r'The complete expansion includes slow variation of the background spectral parameters. The notebook\textquotesingle s single-theta formula would vanish in genus zero, although the free Fourier modes do not.',literal='false as written',match='partial')
corr('P001087','equation (3), variational annotation',r'I_3=\tfrac14(u_x^2+u^3),\quad \delta I_3/\delta u=\tfrac14u_{xx}-\tfrac34u^2',r'For the written density the derivative is -\tfrac12u_{xx}+\tfrac34u^2; the displayed flow instead uses \int(-u_x^2/8-u^3/4)dx.','Scan and both texts agree; integration by parts gives the discrepancy.')
corr('P001088','equation (10)',r'(\delta e_s)^2=|\langle\delta u\Psi^2\rangle|^2/\langle|\Psi|^2\rangle','Specify normalization and use the two-dimensional eigenvalue perturbation matrix; a scale-invariant single-mode formula needs the squared norm in the denominator.','Scan confirms no outer square. Rescaling the Bloch eigenfunction changes the written expression; free Fourier perturbation also fixes the full-gap factor 4.')
corr('P001089','equation (12)','Second derivative of log of a single shifted theta function times an exponential','Use the theta quotient and the Abel shifts in Krichever1983 Lemma 3, eq.(13).','The notebook and transcriptions agree. The printed lemma and genus-zero limit disprove the literal formula.')
corr('P001089','equation (13)','Both factors carry s while the condition is s != s-prime','Distinguish the paired mode indices, as in Krichever1983 Lemma 3.','Scan crop orthogonality.png confirms the condition and the unresolved repeated index; no confident claim of an OCR error.','ambiguity')
save()
# Record 587
r=587
talk(r,'1983-02-28','Mariusz Wodzicki','P001088-P001094','Finite gap Hill zeta functions and regularized determinants',r'From the lower part of P001089 through P001092, Wodzicki presents zeta values for finite gap Hill operators, universal polynomials, and a Lam\'e example. Interventions concern elementary zeta values, arithmetic, determinant multiplicativity, and a Clausen-series formula. The core became a three-page 1984 paper; the peripheral questions have different evidentiary status.')
item(r,'P001089 (lower portion)','definition','Shifted spectral zeta function of a Hill operator',r'\(\zeta_{L-w}(s)=\sum(\lambda-w)^{-s}\), initially \(\Re s>1/2\); the question concerns positive integer values for a finite gap potential and spectral \(w\).',r'For smooth real \(T\)-periodic \(u\), let \(L=-\partial_x^2+u\) have periodic or antiperiodic boundary conditions on \([0,T]\). Remove zero eigenvalues of \(L-w\) and choose a cut for negative eigenvalues when needed. The spectral series converges for \(\Re s>1/2\) and continues meromorphically. At spectral shifts the deleted zero eigenspace and boundary condition are part of the data.',wt,[W],cls='A',reconstruction=r'Boundary conditions, a spectral cut, and the prime on the sum are essential additions. The short publication treats the simple and double spectra of both boundary problems.')
item(r,'P001089','remark','Hurwitz and Epstein comparisons and a binomial expansion',r'Gelfand compares \(\sum(n+a)^{-s}\) and \(\sum(n^2+a)^{-s}\), mentions functional equations, and writes a malformed expansion.',r'For \(a>0\), the Hurwitz zeta function is \(\zeta(s,a)=\sum_{n\ge0}(n+a)^{-s}\). For \(|a|<n^2\), \((n^2+a)^{-s}=n^{-2s}\sum_{j\ge0}(-1)^j(s)_j a^j/(j!n^{2j})\). Classical Epstein functional equations concern positive definite quadratic forms; an arbitrary homogeneous polynomial is not covered merely by homogeneity.',r'These are older special-function comparisons, not the new finite gap theorem. The authoritative formulas for Hurwitz zeta and its continuation are collected in \citet[\S25.11]{nist2026dlmf}; the expansion also follows directly from the binomial theorem. The handwritten date 1899 is not used as a priority claim.',['nist2026dlmf'],literal='false as written',match='partial',speaker='I. M. Gelfand')
item(r,'P001089','question','Arithmetic of shifted spectral values',r'The discussion asks what happens for rational \(a\) and refers to arithmetic features of the resulting zeta series.',r'No particular property---rationality, algebraicity, irrationality, special value, or functional equation---is fixed, and the shift and boundary conditions are not specified consistently enough to extract a single arithmetic problem.',r'Neither the located finite gap paper nor its Lam\'e example states the blanket arithmetic question suggested by the discussion. We retain the question as unidentifiable, without attaching a famous irrationality conjecture.',[W],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',status='As of November 2026, no definite proposition can be assigned to this wording.')
item(r,'P001090-P001091','construction','Universal polynomials and positive integer zeta values',r'The notes define \(F(u)=1+\sum c_i u^i/i!\), polynomial coefficients from \((u\partial_u+p)F^p\), renormalized \(\widehat Q_{p,k}\), and local derivatives of the quasimomentum; equation (4) expresses zeta values using them and Bernoulli numbers.',r'In the finite gap setting the Taylor coefficients of the normalized quasimomentum at a branch point determine all positive integer shifted zeta values by universal polynomials. A normalization-independent computational formulation is to differentiate the regularized spectral determinant: off the spectrum, \(\partial_w^k\zeta_{L-w}\prime(0)=(k-1)!\zeta_{L-w}(k)\). At an eigenvalue, remove the appropriate zero factor before taking the finite part. The four simple/double and periodic/antiperiodic cases have different coefficients.',r'\citet[\S2, Theorem~2 and its proof]{wodzicki1984values} gives the universal-polynomial formulas and derives them from Theorem~1. It does not support using the notebook\textquotesingle s displayed coefficients independently of its unrecorded normalization.',[W],cls='A',reconstruction=r'The derivative identity gives an exact modern statement without guessing ambiguous factorials in the written polynomial definition. The printed \(p=0\) convention and the power-series convention must be read together.',match='partial',literal='not determinable')
item(r,'P001091','claim','The free circle and Bernoulli normalization',r'The notes say that \(u=0,T=2\pi\) gives \(2\zeta(2s)\), then state a Bernoulli formula without the corresponding period factors.',r'For \(-\partial_x^2\) on a circle of length \(T\), with the zero mode removed, \(\zeta_L(s)=2(T/2\pi)^{2s}\zeta_{\mathbb R}(2s)\). Consequently \(\zeta_L(m)=T^{2m}|B_{2m}|/(2m)!\) for positive integers \(m\). In particular, at \(T=2\pi\) the factor is \((2\pi)^{2m}\), not \(2^{2m}\).',r'This is the elementary free check of the formulas in \citet[Theorem~2]{wodzicki1984values}; Euler\textquotesingle s value formula is given in \citet[\S25.6]{nist2026dlmf}.',[W,'nist2026dlmf'],literal='false as written')
item(r,'P001091','claim','One-gap Lamé quasimomentum',r'For \(u=2\wp(x)\), the notes write a cubic spectral curve, a Weierstrass-zeta Bloch exponent, and a quasimomentum differential.',r'Choose a real nonsingular periodic translate \(u(x)=2\wp(x+x_0)\). If the elliptic curve for \(\wp\) is \(y^2=4\prod(z-e_i)\), the Hill spectral curve uses \(E=-z\), hence its branch points are \(-e_i\). The Bloch exponent is obtained from \(T\zeta(a)-\eta a\), with \(\wp(a)=-E\), and the differential and zeta values follow after this coordinate change.',r'\citet[\S4]{wodzicki1984values} explicitly distinguishes the Weierstrass curve from the reflected spectral curve and gives the simple and double spectral values.',[W],cls='A',reconstruction=r'The real-axis translate avoids poles. A \(+g_3\) in the spectral cubic and a \(-g_3\) in the Weierstrass cubic are compatible under \(E=-z\); the sign is not automatically a transcription error.',match='exact')
item(r,'P001092','question','Multiplicativity of the zeta determinant',r'Gelfand asks whether regularized determinants of differential operators are multiplicative.',r'For commuting, positive, invertible, classical elliptic pseudodifferential operators \(A,B\) of positive orders \(a,b\), on a closed smooth manifold and on the same Hermitian vector bundle, define \(\det_\zeta A=\exp[-\zeta_A\prime(0)]\). In general \(\det_\zeta(AB)\ne\det_\zeta A\det_\zeta B\). In this commuting positive setting the logarithmic anomaly is \[\frac{\operatorname{res}\bigl(\log(A^bB^{-a})\bigr)^2}{2ab(a+b)}.\] Noncommuting or nonpositive operators require the appropriate more general formula and compatible admissible cuts.',r'\citet[\S1.1]{kontsevichvishik1994geometry} explains the anomaly and the spectral-cut hypotheses; its June 1994 preprint is an abridgement of their April 1994 work. The commuting formula is explicitly stated with its hypotheses in \citet[eq.~(1)]{castillogarate2015anomaly}.',[ 'kontsevichvishik1994geometry','castillogarate2015anomaly'],cls='B',problem='GP-1982-40',status='As of November 2026, general multiplicativity is false. Local residue anomaly formulas and special vanishing classes are established; there is no remaining blanket conjecture here.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(r,'P001092','remark','Euler, Apéry, and regularized sums',r'The discussion mentions Euler\textquotesingle s even values, Ap\'ery\textquotesingle s \(\zeta(3)\), and assigns values to \(\sum n^2\), \(\sum n^3\).',r'Euler\textquotesingle s formula is \(\zeta(2m)=(-1)^{m+1}B_{2m}(2\pi)^{2m}/[2(2m)!]\). Ap\'ery proved that \(\zeta(3)\) is irrational. Analytic continuation gives \(\zeta(-2)=0\) and \(\zeta(-3)=1/120=-B_4/4\); these are zeta-regularized values, not sums of convergent positive-term series.',r'\citet{apery1979irrationalite} published the irrationality proof in 1979, before the talk. The continuation identities are recorded in \citet[\S25.6]{nist2026dlmf}.',['apery1979irrationalite','nist2026dlmf'],literal='false as written')
item(r,'P001092','remark','The D-module sketch for a complex power',r'A short diagram separates integer and noninteger exponents of \(x^{-s}\), with arrows and subquotients.',r'On the punctured complex line, \(x^{-s}\) generates the rank-one connection \((x\partial_x+s)f=0\) with monodromy \(e^{-2\pi i s}\). At integral exponents, extensions across zero can have punctual subquotients. A specific extension functor or module presentation is needed to identify the diagram.',r'The local connection is an elementary reconstruction; the notebook does not specify the extension or its arrows sufficiently to match an exact module theorem. No fate is inferred from a general reference on D-modules.',[],cls='F',reconstruction=r'The rank-one connection is supplied only to locate the subject. It is not an assertion that the diagram equals a specified minimal or maximal extension.',status='As of November 2026, the local connection is standard; the displayed extension diagram remains unidentifiable.',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low')
item(r,'P001092','claim','Clausen series and a negative-even zeta derivative',r'The notes express \(\zeta_{L-w}\prime(-2)\) using Clausen functions and branch-count corrections; they write \(\operatorname{Cl}_5(0)=\zeta(-5)\).',r'With \(\operatorname{Cl}_{2k+1}(\theta)=\sum_{n\ge1}\cos(n\theta)/n^{2k+1}\), one has \(\operatorname{Cl}_5(0)=\zeta(5)\). For the free circle, \(\zeta_L\prime(-2)=48\zeta(5)/T^4\), obtained by differentiating \(2(T/2\pi)^{2s}\zeta(2s)\). The general shifted formula needs the spectral branch and the counting function for negative eigenvalues.',r'The located Wodzicki paper proves positive integer values, not the entire Clausen expression on this page. The free specialization follows from \citet[\S25.4]{nist2026dlmf}; the complete displayed identity has not been verified in an exact source.',['nist2026dlmf',W],cls='F',reconstruction=r'\oursComputed{} Only the explicitly checked free specialization and the definition of the Clausen function are asserted here.',status='As of November 2026, the free specialization is established; the full recorded branch-sensitive formula remains unverified.',literal='false as written',match='partial',confidence='medium')
