talk(r,'1983-03-21','S. P. Novikov','P001092-P001095','Many-valued actions and transgression',r'The talk occupies P001093 and the upper part of P001094. It constructs a globally defined variation from locally defined action functionals, illustrated by the Dirac monopole and mapping spaces. The 1982 survey is already a pre-talk source.')
item(r,'P001093','construction','A closed one-form as a many-valued action',r'The action is \(S=S_0+S_1\), where local \(q\)-form primitives of a closed \((q+1)\)-form define \(S_1\). The variation is asserted to be single-valued.',r'Let \(N\) be a closed oriented smooth \(q\)-manifold and \(\Omega\in\Omega^{q+1}(M)\) closed. On the smooth mapping space the transgression \(\alpha_\phi(v)=\int_N\phi^*(\iota_v\Omega)\) is a closed one-form. Locally it is the derivative of an action; its periods are integrals of \(\Omega\) over the cycles swept out by loops of maps. Adding a single-valued functional \(S_0\) does not change this period obstruction.',r'\citet[\S5]{novikov1982hamiltonian} gives the many-valued functional construction. It was received 22 April 1982 and published in 1982, before this March 1983 exposition.',[N],reconstruction=r'Closedness and orientation of the source, and the transgression formula, are explicit modern hypotheses. The notebook does not develop a complete differential-cohomology model.')
item(r,'P001093-P001094','claim','Dirac monopole and the phase of the action',r'The example takes \(N=S^1\), \(M=S^2\), and a closed two-form that is not globally exact.',r'For the magnetic two-form of a monopole on \(S^2\), local magnetic potentials give a multi-valued action on loops. The curvature can have a global primitive only if its integral over \(S^2\) vanishes. If the transgression periods lie in \(2\pi\mathbb Z\), exponentiating the action gives a single-valued phase on the chosen component, after a reference normalization.',r'\citet[\S\S4--5]{novikov1982hamiltonian} explicitly treats the Dirac monopole and the resulting multivalued action. The integral-period phase formulation is a reconstruction of the obstruction, not a separate theorem claimed from the seminar.',[N])
item(r,'P001094 (upper portion)','claim','Local actions and constant transition differences',r'The notes choose primitive patches \(U_\alpha\) in \(M\), use maps whose entire images lie in one patch, and say \(S_\alpha-S_\beta\) is constant. A tentative Witten/chiral-field reference follows.',r'On an overlap of mapping-space domains where two local actions have the same derivative, their difference is constant on each connected component. Domains consisting of maps into one primitive patch need not cover the entire mapping space; a general construction uses local homotopies to reference maps or subdivisions. The chiral-field attribution does not state a further definite claim.',r'\citet[\S5, the local-action construction]{novikov1982hamiltonian} explains why the variations agree. The scope repair follows from the closed transgression form, without asserting that every map fits into one target chart.',[N],reconstruction=r'Locally constant replaces globally constant on a disconnected overlap. The tentative attribution is retained as a bibliographic lead, not an exact later solution.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
corr('P001094','local action covering','Every map is represented by a map into one primitive patch','Use local domains in the mapping space; one-target-patch domains need not cover it. Differences of local primitives are locally constant.','The scan shows a target-space covering. The general construction requires local homotopies or subdivision.','faithful_error')
save()
# Record 589
r=589
talk(r,'1983-03-21','M. A. Semenov-Tian-Shansky','P001093-P001097','Classical r-matrices and factorization',r'From the second heading on P001094 through P001095 the notes introduce an operator \(R\), two Lie--Poisson brackets, invariant Hamiltonians, modified Yang--Baxter identities and factorization. Gelfand asks for the \(\mathfrak{sl}_2\) classification. The final quadratic bracket anticipates the exact 1983 article.')
item(r,'P001094 (lower portion)','definition','Double Lie algebras and invariant Hamiltonians',r'\([X,Y]_R=[RX,Y]+[X,RY]\) is required to be a Lie bracket; invariant functions commute for both associated brackets, and their Hamiltonian equation has a Lax form.',r'If a linear map \(R:\mathfrak g\to\mathfrak g\) makes \([\ ,\ ]_R\) a Lie bracket, the two brackets define Lie--Poisson structures on \(\mathfrak g^*\). Adjoint-invariant smooth functions are in involution for both. For such \(h\), the second Hamiltonian vector field is \(\dot L=\operatorname{ad}^*_{R\,dh(L)}L\), with the coadjoint sign convention fixed consistently. A double Lie algebra alone is not the same datum as a Lie bialgebra.',st+r' The exact result is \citet[\S1, Theorem~1; \S2]{semenov1983classical}.',[S],cls='A',reconstruction=r'Finite dimensionality is sufficient for the stated differential-geometric formulation; infinite-dimensional examples require a specified pairing and domains.')
item(r,'P001094-P001095','method','Modified Yang–Baxter equation, the double, and factorization',r'The defect \([RX,RY]-R([RX,Y]+[X,RY])\) is set equal to \(-[X,Y]\). The maps \(R\pm1\), a decomposition in the double, and the factorization of \(\exp(t\,dh)\) are used to integrate the flow.',r'The modified identity implies that \(R\pm I:\mathfrak g_R\to\mathfrak g\) are Lie homomorphisms. Their joint image in \(\mathfrak g\oplus\mathfrak g\) is complementary to the diagonal. In the corresponding local groups, the factorization problem, with its common-quotient constraint, is locally soluble. For the unhalved bracket used here, the exponential is \(\exp(2t\,dh(L_0))\); the resulting coadjoint action integrates the invariant Hamiltonian flow.',r'\citet[\S\S3--4, Proposition~9 and Theorem~11, eq.~(18)]{semenov1983classical} gives the exact local result, including the factor \(2\). '+st,[S],cls='A',literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'Global factorization is not automatic. The notebook omits the quotient compatibility when the two images intersect and loses the factor of two associated with its bracket convention.')
item(r,'P001095','question','Gelfand’s classification problem for sl(2)',r'Gelfand asks to find all \(R\) satisfying the displayed modified Yang--Baxter equation on \(\mathfrak{sl}_2\).',r'Over \(\mathbb C\), classify all linear maps \(R\) satisfying \([Rx,Ry]-R([Rx,y]+[x,Ry])=-[x,y]\), modulo Lie-algebra automorphisms. No skew-adjoint restriction is imposed by the question. Writing \(B=(R-I)/2\) identifies this exactly with Rota--Baxter operators of weight one: \([Bx,By]=B([Bx,y]+[x,By]+[x,y])\).',r'An explicit classification and proof are in \citet[Proposition~2.5 and its proof]{gubarevkozlov2023rota}; its source cases are closed under conjugacy and the involution \(B\mapsto-B-I\), with \(0,-I\) included separately. Received September 2022, published 2023. This answers the complex operator problem without replacing it by the narrower skew tensor problem.',[S,'gubarevkozlov2023rota'],cls='B',problem='GP-1982-41',reconstruction=r'The ground field and equivalence relation are supplied. Real forms require a separate classification, which the notebook does not select.',status='As of November 2026, the complex finite-dimensional operator classification is established. The primary proof was read; this item does not claim a new classification.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',confidence='high')
item(r,'P001095','construction','Quadratic Poisson bracket and the Hamiltonian hierarchy',r'A quadratic bracket with terms \(R(XL)\) and \(R(LX)\) is followed by a hierarchy of invariant Hamiltonians.',r'Let \(\mathfrak g\) be an associative algebra with nondegenerate symmetric invariant trace pairing, and let \(R\) be skew with respect to it and satisfy the modified Yang--Baxter equation. Then the quadratic bracket of Semenov-Tian-Shansky\textquotesingle s equation (22) is Poisson and compatible with the linear \(R\)-bracket. Invariant functions commute for it, and trace powers produce the associated Lenard hierarchy. In formal pseudodifferential examples the trace is the integrated formal residue and the differential/negative-order splitting supplies \(R\).',r'\citet[\S5, Theorem~13 and eqs.~(22)--(23)]{semenov1983classical} gives precisely these hypotheses. This is related to earlier Gelfand--Dikii discussions (1977-426.5 and 1977-426.10), but not an exact recurrence of the earlier question about the first bracket.',[S],cls='A',reconstruction=r'Skew-adjointness, the trace pairing and associative multiplication are needed for the particular theorem verified here; the bare assumption that the linear \(R\)-bracket satisfies Jacobi is insufficient.')
prob('GP-1982-41','Classical modified Yang–Baxter operators on sl(2)',r'Classify complex-linear \(R:\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C)\to\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C)\) with modified Yang--Baxter defect \(-[x,y]\), up to Lie-algebra automorphism.',['1982-589.3'],r'November 2026: solved via the equivalent weight-one Rota--Baxter classification; no skew restriction may be silently imposed.',['gubarevkozlov2023rota',S])
corr('P001095','factorization exponential',r'\exp(t\,dh(L_0))',r'\exp(2t\,dh(L_0)) for the unhalved R-bracket','Semenov1983 Theorem 11 eq.(18) explicitly has 2t; the derivative at t=0 gives the same check.')
save()
# Record 590
r=590
talk(r,'1983-03-28','Mariusz Wodzicki','P001095-P001099','APS boundary index theory and local spectral invariants',r'The actual record is P001096--P001098. It reviews all three Atiyah--Patodi--Singer papers, includes Fedosov\textquotesingle s symbol-projector construction, and connects heat coefficients, eta regularity and secondary invariants. Gelfand asks about formal cohomology of metrics and symbols. Several questions recur verbatim in the 1981--82 index.')
item(r,'P001096','theorem','Spectral boundary conditions and the APS index formula',r'For \(D=\partial_u+A\) near the boundary, the projector onto the nonnegative spectrum of self-adjoint \(A\) is imposed; the index is \(\int_X\alpha_0-(h+\eta(0))/2\).',r'Let a Dirac-type elliptic operator on a compact manifold with boundary have product structure in a collar, with self-adjoint tangential operator \(A\). Impose \(P_{\ge0}(A)(s|_{\partial X})=0\). The realization is Fredholm and \(\operatorname{ind}D_{APS}=\int_X\alpha_0-(\eta_A(0)+\dim\ker A)/2\). The normal bundle isomorphism in the usual Dirac decomposition is suppressed in the notes.',r'\citet[Theorem~3.10 and \S4]{atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1} is the exact pre-talk source (received March 1974, published 1975). This repeats 1981-558.11--12 and 1981-559.2.',[APS])
item(r,'P001096','question','Replacing self-adjointness by a spectral-cut hypothesis',r'The notes say self-adjointness may be replaced by absence of spectrum near the imaginary axis.',r'Complex powers of a general elliptic classical operator require an Agmon ray: the ray avoids both the nonzero principal-symbol eigenvalues and the operator spectrum. For boundary problems the corresponding sectorial projection must also define an elliptic boundary condition. Avoiding the imaginary axis only at the level of the discrete global spectrum does not supply these microlocal hypotheses.',r'\citet[\S1, Agmon--Nirenberg condition]{kontsevichvishik1994geometry} states the required symbol condition explicitly. Wodzicki\textquotesingle s earlier \citet{wodzicki1982spectral} is an exact spectral-asymmetry lead, but only its publisher metadata was checked in this run. This is the earlier problem GP-1981-44.', ['kontsevichvishik1994geometry','wodzicki1982spectral'],cls='B',problem='GP-1981-44',reconstruction=r'The statement separates the established spectral-cut calculus from a blanket boundary Fredholm theorem, which the one-line remark does not formulate.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(r,'P001096-P001097','construction','Fedosov’s idempotents for an elliptic symbol',r'Fedosov constructs a matrix projector from cutoffs \(f_1,f_2\) and \(\sigma,\sigma^*\), then gives a pair \(P_1,P_2\) for general bundles.',r'An elliptic symbol \(\sigma:\pi^*E_1\to\pi^*E_2\), invertible away from the zero section, defines a compactly supported \(K^0(T^*X)\) class. In a unitary trivialization the block matrix \(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}f_1^2&f_1f_2\sigma\\f_1f_2\sigma^*&f_2^2\end{smallmatrix}\right)\) is an idempotent when \(\sigma\sigma^*=I\) and \(f_1^2+f_2^2=1\). For the later nonorthogonal idempotent pair, the auxiliary symbol and its parametrix must be mutual inverses outside a compact set for their difference to represent a compactly supported class.',r'The symbol-class and Chern-character framework is the classical index construction described in \citet[Chapter~3]{gilkey1984invariance}. The written projector identities were checked directly; the exact displayed cutoff formula was not attributed to an independently located Fedosov paper.',[G],reconstruction=r'The algebraic projector identities and the support condition are separate issues. We do not assert that the later pair fails idempotence; the potential error is its cutoff behavior at infinity.',literal='false as written',match='partial',speaker='B. V. Fedosov',confidence='medium')
item(r,'P001097','method','Chern character, Todd form, and the symbol index',r'The displayed integral is a difference of traces of \(P_i\exp(\omega_i/2\pi i)\) multiplied by the Todd class; \(\omega_i=P_i dP_i dP_i\).',r'For smooth idempotents representing a compactly supported symbol class, the Grassmann connection has curvature \(P(dP)^2\). Its Chern character difference is paired with \(\pi^*\operatorname{Td}(T_\mathbb C X)\), using the cotangent orientation and the conventional index sign, to obtain the analytic index. Curvature normalization and complexification matter in comparing \(R/(e^R-1)\) with \(R/(1-e^{-R})\).',r'This is classical topological index theory; \citet[Chapter~3]{gilkey1984invariance} supplies the Chern--Weil formulation. No new universal Todd identity is inferred from the crossed-out curvature notation.',[G],speaker='B. V. Fedosov',reconstruction=r'The support, orientation and curvature conventions have been supplied; the notebook\textquotesingle s two Todd conventions are not treated as an unambiguous sign error.')
item(r,'P001097','method','Index from shifted zeta functions and local parametrices',r'Wodzicki sets \(DD^*=\Delta_2\), \(D^*D=\Delta_1\), writes an index difference of shifted zeta values, and a local symbol integral for \(\alpha_0\).',r'On a closed manifold, \(\operatorname{ind}D=\zeta_{D^*D+1}(0)-\zeta_{DD^*+1}(0)\), because the positive spectra cancel and each zero mode becomes the eigenvalue one. For a positive elliptic differential operator \(A\), \(\zeta_A(0)\) with zero modes removed equals its integrated constant heat coefficient minus \(\dim\ker A\). Resolvent-symbol contour integrals compute the heat coefficients, with an admissible cut and the chosen Fourier normalization.',r'\citet[\S1.10 and Chapter~3]{gilkey1984invariance} gives the heat/zeta formulation. This is a recurrence of 1981-558.3--4, 1981-559.10--11; the first argument on the scan is an ambiguous crossed zero, not a new parameter.',[G],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'The exact shifted identity concerns a closed elliptic problem, or paired boundary realizations with the required spectral cancellation. A generic pseudodifferential heat expansion may include logarithmic terms, so the differential-operator hypothesis is retained.')
item(r,'P001098','theorem','Local index densities for signature and Dirac operators',r'The notebook says the local density agrees with the topological formula for signature and Dirac operators and mentions equivariant versions.',r'For geometric signature and Dirac-type complexes on a closed Riemannian manifold, the supertrace heat-kernel constant term is the appropriate characteristic-form density, with the twisting Chern character when present. For an orientation-preserving compact-group symmetry preserving the operator data, the equivariant index is described by fixed-point local contributions.',r'The pre-talk local index theorem is proved in \citet{atiyahbottpatodi1973heat}; \citet[Chapter~3]{gilkey1984invariance} provides the corresponding invariant-theoretic treatment. This repeats 1981-558.1 and 1981-558.7.', ['atiyahbottpatodi1973heat',G])
item(r,'P001098','question','Formal cohomology of metrics and symbols',r'Gelfand proposes calculating formal cohomology for the metric and the symbol and asks whether classes other than the index occur; Gelfand--Fuks cohomology and maximal topological invariants are mentioned.',r'The note does not specify the Lie algebra of formal coordinate changes, coefficient module, equivalence relation on symbols, or degree. Those choices distinguish Chern--Weil invariant polynomials, local scalar invariants, and Gelfand--Fuks cohomology, so no single formal-cohomology calculation is determined.',r'\citet[\S\S2.4--2.6]{gilkey1984invariance} classifies specified local invariant problems; it is thematic evidence, not a solution of the unspecified combined metric-and-symbol problem.',[G],cls='F',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',match='thematic only',literal='not determinable',confidence='low',status='As of November 2026, no definite open question can be extracted without choosing the formal complex.')
item(r,'P001098','theorem','Signature defect and equivariant eta series',r'The signature formula is \(\operatorname{sign}X=\int_XL-\eta(0)\); an equivariant eta series inserts \(\operatorname{Tr}(g|E_\lambda)\).',r'For a compact oriented \(4k\)-manifold with product metric near its boundary, the APS signature theorem expresses the signature as the integral of the Hirzebruch \(L\)-form minus the eta invariant of the boundary odd signature operator in the APS convention. If \(g\) is a symmetry commuting with the self-adjoint tangential operator, \(\eta_A(s,g)=\sum_{\lambda\ne0}\operatorname{sign}(\lambda)\operatorname{Tr}(g|E_\lambda)|\lambda|^{-s}\) initially converges in a right half-plane.',r'\citet[\S4]{atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1} gives the signature theorem; the boundary operator uses the Hodge star, not merely the formal adjoint of \(d\). This continues 1981-558.8.',[APS],reconstruction=r'The signature normalization differs from the general index expression with \((\eta+h)/2\); its kernel convention cannot simply be interchanged.')
item(r,'P001098','theorem','Eta regularity without a bounding manifold',r'Part III is summarized as regularity of \(\eta(0)\) in odd dimension without requiring the operator to be a boundary operator.',r'For a self-adjoint classical elliptic operator of positive order on a closed smooth manifold, the global eta function is regular at zero. APS III proves the odd-dimensional result by its topological argument; the general-dimensional theorem does not imply that the local eta residue density vanishes pointwise.',r'\citet[Theorem~4.5]{atiyahpatodisinger1976spectral3} proves the odd-dimensional statement (received January 1975, published 1976). \citet[Theorem~0.1]{gilkey1981residue} proves the general statement before this 1983 talk. This settles the exact earlier problem GP-1981-43.', ['atiyahpatodisinger1976spectral3','gilkey1981residue'],problem='GP-1981-43',status='As of November 2026, the global eta-regularity question is settled; local residue assertions require separate hypotheses.')
item(r,'P001098 (bottom)','construction','Flat bundles, secondary K-theory, and reduced eta',r'A unitary representation of \(\pi_1X\), a class with \(\mathbb R/\mathbb Z\) coefficients, and a map to \(\mathbb R/\mathbb Z\) are drawn next to the symbol class.',r'A rank-\(n\) flat unitary bundle defines a secondary class relative to the trivial rank-\(n\) bundle. Pairing an appropriate \(K\)-theory class with it gives a secondary index in \(\mathbb R/\mathbb Z\), represented analytically by the difference of reduced eta invariants \(\bar\eta(A_E)-n\bar\eta(A)\) modulo integers. The base class lies on \(X\); its product with a compactly supported cotangent symbol is formed only after pullback.',r'\citet[\S\S4.3--4.4]{gilkey1984invariance} gives the secondary-index and eta interpretation of APS theory. The notebook\textquotesingle s diagram is schematic; the exact coefficient grading is not recoverable from its arrows alone.',[G],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',reconstruction=r'Reduced eta means \((\eta(0)+\dim\ker)/2\). We supply the rank subtraction and distinguish a class on the base from its pullback to the cotangent bundle.',confidence='medium')
prob('GP-1981-44','Spectral zeta constructions without self-adjointness',r'Extend complex-power, zeta and spectral-asymmetry constructions beyond self-adjoint elliptic operators under suitable spectral-cut hypotheses.',['1981-559.8','1981-559.13','1981-559.14','1982-590.2'],r'November 2026: complex powers and zeta functions are established for admissible elliptic operators; the cut must avoid principal-symbol spectrum as well as global spectrum. Boundary realizations need their own ellipticity conditions.',['kontsevichvishik1994geometry','wodzicki1982spectral'])
prob('GP-1981-43','Eta regularity without a bounding manifold',r'Is the global eta function regular at zero for a self-adjoint elliptic operator without assuming it bounds an elliptic complex?',['1981-559.9','1982-590.9'],r'November 2026: yes for closed classical elliptic operators of positive order; APS III treats odd dimension and Gilkey1981 supplies the general-dimensional result. Local residue densities need not vanish.',['atiyahpatodisinger1976spectral3','gilkey1981residue'])
corr('P001096','Fedosov block projector',r'f_1+f_2=1',r'f_1^2+f_2^2=1, with a unitary symbol for the displayed adjoint blocks','Multiplying the displayed block matrix gives P^2=(f1^2+f2^2)P under unitary normalization. Scan confirms the unsquared written partition.')
corr('P001097','auxiliary symbol a and cutoff',r'a=f_1\sigma,\quad\tau=f_2\sigma^{-1}','If the reading f1 is retained, replace its infinity behavior: a and tau must be inverse outside a compact set. A likely intended choice is a=f2 sigma.','The f1 subscript is ambiguous in crop cutoff1097b.png. Under the preceding cutoff definitions the literal f1 choice destroys compact support of P1-P2; idempotence itself still holds.','ambiguity')
corr('P001097','first shifted zeta argument',r'\zeta_{\Delta_1+1}(\theta)',r'\zeta_{\Delta_1+1}(0)','The mark is a crossed or overwritten zero. Only zero gives the index formula stated in context.','ambiguity')
corr('P001098','boundary signature operator',r'\pm(d^*-d)','Use the odd signature operator built from d and the Hodge star, with degree-dependent signs.','The scan has a star near d but the shorthand does not specify Hodge star versus formal adjoint. The APS signature formula fixes its intended geometric meaning.','ambiguity')
