# Record 591
r=591
talk(r,'1983-03-28','George Wilson','P001097-P001102','Loop Grassmannians, integrable flows, and the tau function',r'The actual record is P001099--P001100. Wilson announces joint work with Segal and relates loop groups, Grassmannians, KdV, modified KdV, determinant lines and affine central extensions. Krichever adds the zero-curvature KP interpretation. This is the prospectus for the detailed April lecture.')
item(r,'P001099','construction','Loops, Hardy polarization, and the restricted Grassmannian',r'\(H^{(n)}=L^2(S^1,\mathbb C^n)=H_+\oplus H_-\), \(W_\gamma=\gamma H_+\); the positive projection is Fredholm and the negative one compact. The Grassmannian is compared with \(\Omega U(n)\).',r'For a continuous invertible matrix loop \(\gamma\), multiplication on \(L^2\) is bounded invertible, \(W_\gamma\) is closed, and its Hardy projections have the stated properties. The full restricted Grassmannian is larger than the loop orbit. The subspace with \(zW\subset W\) is the appropriate loop Grassmannian; continuous based unitary loops inject into a dense subspace of it, with a completion admitting measurable loops.',r'\citet[\S2, Proposition~2.7 and the following discussion]{segalwilson1985loop} states the continuous-loop inclusion and the distinction between the completed Grassmannian and \(\Omega U(n)\). '+swt,[SW],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(r,'P001099','question','Which measurable loops give the Fredholm and compact projections?',r'The notes call it an unsolved problem to characterize all loops for which conditions (i) and (ii) hold.',r'For continuous invertible loops the conditions already hold. A precise wider version takes an essentially bounded unitary matrix loop \(\gamma\). Then \(W_\gamma\to H_+\) is Fredholm and \(W_\gamma\to H_-\) compact exactly when the matrix entries lie in \(QC=(H^\infty+C)\cap\overline{H^\infty+C}\). Indeed Hartman\textquotesingle s theorem identifies compact Hankel blocks; the Toeplitz block is essentially unitary exactly when both off-diagonal blocks are compact.',r'\citet[pp.~221--223]{page1975compact} proves the compact-Hankel characterization (Hartman\textquotesingle s theorem dates to 1958). \ours{} The unitary-loop equivalence is the elementary block-operator consequence checked here. The notes do not specify this ambient measurable class, so no unresolved general \(GL_n\) loop problem is asserted.',[SW,'page1975compact'],cls='E',reconstruction=r'We explicitly restrict the reconstruction to essentially bounded unitary loops. \ours{} Write the unitary multiplier as \(U=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{smallmatrix}\right)\). If \(c\) is compact, then \(a^*a=1\) in the Calkin algebra; Fredholmness makes \(a\) invertible there, whence \(aa^*=1\) and \(bb^*=0\), so \(b\) is compact. Conversely compact \(b,c\) make \(a\) unitary modulo compacts and hence Fredholm. Hartman\textquotesingle s criterion then gives \(QC\) entrywise.',status='As of November 2026, the unitary measurable reconstruction has the stated classical criterion. The literal phrase all loops lacks a function class.',literal='not determinable',match='partial',confidence='medium')
item(r,'P001099-P001100','construction','Grassmannian flows and Krichever’s algebro-geometric locus',r'The notes map a Grassmannian to solutions of the \(n\)th KdV hierarchy and place the Krichever construction inside it, followed by a dressing-method comparison.',r'For scalar polarized \(L^2(S^1)\), the reduction \(z^nW\subset W\) yields an order-\(n\) scalar differential Lax operator and its Gelfand--Dikii flows. General Grassmannian points give KP. The algebro-geometric construction supplies a distinguished locus, and polynomial/rational finite-dimensional approximations are dense in the relevant Grassmannian topology; this is not a classification of all solutions of arbitrary growth.',r'\citet[\S\S2,5--7]{segalwilson1985loop} verifies the reduction and its algebraic loci. '+swt,[SW],cls='A',reconstruction=r'The reduction and analytic class delimit the word solutions; no universal surjectivity onto every smooth KdV initial value is inferred.')
item(r,'P001100','construction','Modified hierarchies and the Miura flag',r'A commutative diagram relates a flag manifold to modified KdV and the Grassmannian to KdV; the Miura fibre is written \(G/B\).',r'For a local monic scalar differential operator of order \(n\), factorizations into first order operators correspond to suitable complete flags in its local solution space. The Miura transformation carries modified hierarchy solutions to the ordinary hierarchy. Analytic factorizations near an ordinary point form the open set of flags with nonvanishing required Wronskians; allowing meromorphic factors recovers the full flag variety.',r'\citet[\S3.7 and \S6]{drinfeldsokolov1985lie} develops the Miura transformation and generalized hierarchies; its introduction identifies the 1981 Doklady announcement as the earlier source. Thus this is pre-talk mathematics despite the 1984 Russian/1985 English survey publication.', ['drinfeldsokolov1985lie','drinfeldsokolov1981equations'],cls='E',reconstruction=r'Local solution fields and meromorphic versus analytic factorizations are distinguished; global periodic fibres need not equal a single flag variety.')
item(r,'P001100','construction','Determinant lines, affine central extensions, and tau',r'The tau function is introduced as a determinant, with an affine Lie-algebra extension and a theta-function special case.',r'The determinant line over the index-zero restricted Grassmannian produces a tau function after an admissible frame and a lift of the positive loop action are chosen. The restricted linear group acts through a central extension; on smooth matrix loops its infinitesimal cocycle is a scalar multiple of \((2\pi i)^{-1}\oint\operatorname{tr}(X\,dY)\). The scalar tau function is not canonically independent of all trivializations.',r'\citet[\S\S3--4 and 10]{segalwilson1985loop} gives the determinant construction and central extension. The affine algebra itself was older; the specific analytic tau model is the joint work announced here.',[SW],cls='A')
item(r,'P001100 (lower portion)','method','Krichever’s KP zero-curvature and tau reconstruction',r'Krichever replaces commuting ordinary operators by compatible \(\partial_y-L\), \(\partial_t-L_1\), and writes \(u=-2\partial_x^2\log\tau\) for \(L=-\partial_x^2+u\).',r'For a KP auxiliary system, the compatibility condition is \([\partial_y-L,\partial_t-L_1]=0\). In the convention \(L=-\partial_x^2+u\), the potential reconstructed from tau is \(u=-2\partial_x^2\log\tau\), up to any explicitly chosen constant background. The sign reverses for the standard monic \(\partial_x^2+u\) convention.',r'\citet[\S5, especially eq.~(5.19)]{segalwilson1985loop} gives the monic convention and its tau reconstruction. The notebook\textquotesingle s sign is consistent with its negative leading term.',[SW],cls='E',speaker='I. M. Krichever',reconstruction=r'The relation is a compatibility equation in two evolution variables, not ordinary commutativity of time-dependent \(L,L_1\).')
save()
# Record 592
r=592
talk(r,'1983-04-04','George Wilson','P001100-P001109','The determinant tau function and the KP Grassmannian',r'P001101--P001107 gives the detailed Segal--Wilson construction: frames and determinants, Schur expansions, algebraic curves, Baker functions and Miwa shifts, formal Lax equations, determinant-line extensions, and local Miura factorizations. Gelfand\textquotesingle s broad request about theta functions of all abelian varieties is kept separate from the precise Jacobian results.')
item(r,'P001101-P001102','construction','Index-zero Grassmannian and admissible frames',r'The restricted Grassmannian is defined by Fredholm/compact projections. Frames \(w:H_+\to W\) have \(w_+=I+\text{trace class}\), \(w_-\) compact; the text says all points have such frames.',r'The Fredholm index labels the connected components of the restricted Grassmannian. The stated frame condition parametrizes the index-zero component. For \(g\in\Gamma_+\), holomorphic and nonvanishing on a neighbourhood of the closed disc, choose its block decomposition \(g^{-1}=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}a&b\\0&d\end{smallmatrix}\right)\). The trace-class block needed for the determinant is available in this analytic loop class.',r'\citet[\S\S2--3]{segalwilson1985loop} gives the component and frame construction. '+swt,[SW],cls='A',reconstruction=r'The displayed index-zero qualification on the scan must be retained when interpreting the subsequent universal quantifier over points.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(r,'P001102','definition','The Fredholm determinant tau function and its zero set',r'\(\tau_W(g)=\det(w_++a^{-1}bw_-)\); transversality is stated as both invertibility of the positive projection and \(W\cap H_-=0\).',r'On the index-zero component, the operator inside the determinant is identity plus trace class, so its Fredholm determinant is defined. Its vanishing is equivalent to failure of \(g^{-1}W\to H_+\) to be invertible. There, injectivity is equivalent to invertibility because the index is zero. On the big cell the graph frame gives \(\tau_W(1)=1\); elsewhere this normalization is unavailable.',r'\citet[\S3, determinant-line discussion]{segalwilson1985loop} gives this construction.',[SW],cls='A',reconstruction=r'An arbitrary Fredholm operator has no ordinary determinant. The frame normalization and trace-class subtraction are essential.')
item(r,'P001102-P001103','theorem','Nonvanishing, Schur functions, and Plücker coefficients',r'The tau function is never identically zero, even on \(g=e^{xz}\). Coordinate subspaces \(H_S\) give Schur functions, and general tau has a Pl\"ucker-coordinate expansion.',r'For an index-zero Grassmannian point, \(\tau_W(t)=\sum_S w^S F_S(t)\), where \(F_S\) is the Schur function associated with the charge-zero indexing set \(S\), with the sign convention determined by \(g^{-1}\). For \(t=(x,0,\ldots)\), the leading power has nonzero coefficient and degree equal to the codimension of the stratum containing \(W\). Schur functions also encode polynomial \(GL_n\) characters after a finite-variable specialization.',r'\citet[Propositions~8.2--8.3 and 8.6]{segalwilson1985loop} proves the expansion and the stronger one-variable nonvanishing. The representation-theoretic identification answers the brief \(GL(n)\) aside.',[SW],cls='A',reconstruction=r'The coefficient conventions use the charge-zero partition, not an arbitrary increasing set without its eventual indexing condition.')
item(r,'P001103','construction','The Krichever map and its Fredholm index',r'A pointed curve, a disc at infinity, a line bundle (rank-one torsion-free sheaf for a singular curve), and a trivialization produce \(W\); the index is \(\chi(\mathcal L)-1\).',r'Let \(X\) be an integral complete algebraic curve, smooth at a marked point \(p\), with a local coordinate and a trivialized rank-one torsion-free sheaf \(\mathcal L\) near \(p\). The closure of boundary values of sections on the complement defines a Grassmannian point. With \(H_+=\operatorname{span}\{1,z,z^2,\ldots\}\) and \(z\) a pole coordinate at \(p\), the projection index is \(\chi(\mathcal L(-p))=\chi(\mathcal L)-1\).',r'\citet[\S6, Krichever construction]{segalwilson1985loop} is the exact analytic realization of the older Krichever construction.',[SW],cls='A',reconstruction=r'The coordinate, local freeness at the marked point, and boundary-value completion are supplied. Genus and degree need not be guessed from the index.')
item(r,'P001103-P001104','theorem','Theta factorization of an algebro-geometric tau function',r'The notes relate \(\Gamma_+\) to \(H^1(X,\mathcal O)\) and write \(\tau=e^{\alpha_{W,0}+Q}\theta(\bar f-\beta_W)\), with \(Q\) depending only on the curve and disc.',r'For a smooth pointed curve with fixed local coordinate and line-bundle data, the Krichever tau function is a theta function on its Jacobian composed with the linear map from flow times, multiplied by an exponential with linear and quadratic terms. The quadratic form is determined by the pointed curve and normalization; the line bundle changes the translate and the linear factor.',r'\citet[\S9, especially Theorem~9.11]{segalwilson1985loop} gives the exact theta comparison. This continues the curve-based theta constructions indexed in 1977-431.3.',[SW],cls='A')
item(r,'P001104','question','Gelfand’s request for theta functions of all abelian varieties',r'\ru{Построить теорию интегрирования, связанную с \(\theta\)-функцией \(\forall\) абелевых многообразий}; the parenthesis says only Jacobians of curves occur here.',r'The notes ask for an integration theory attached to arbitrary abelian varieties but do not specify equations, a notion of integrability, or a universal construction. The exact Jacobian theta solution of KP is a different, narrower statement.',r'The exact curve construction is in \citet[\S9]{segalwilson1985loop}. \citet{shiota1986characterization} is a later Jacobian-characterization reference, not a solution of the all-abelian-varieties programme; only its publisher metadata and bibliography were checked here. That bibliography records 1983 Kyoto seminar notes separately from the 1986 publication.',[SW,'shiota1986characterization'],cls='F',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',match='thematic only',literal='not determinable',verification='publisher metadata checked',status='As of November 2026, this broad programme does not specify one checkable open problem; no full solution is attributed to the Schottky/KP literature.',confidence='medium')
item(r,'P001104-P001105','construction','Baker function, tau ratio, and Miwa shifts',r'\(\psi_W(g,z)=g(z)(1+\sum a_i(g)z^{-i})\in W\); multiplying by \(q_\zeta=1-z/\zeta\) gives its tau quotient and a vertex operator.',r'Whenever \(g^{-1}W\to H_+\) is invertible, there is a unique Baker function with the stated asymptotic form and membership in \(W\). Its reduced form is \(\widetilde\psi_W(g,\zeta)=\tau_W(gq_\zeta)/\tau_W(g)\), \(|\zeta|>1\). If \(g=\exp(\sum t_i z^i)\), this is the shift \(t_i\mapsto t_i-1/(i\zeta^i)\), and hence a differential-exponential vertex-operator formula. The coefficients extend meromorphically through the tau zero set.',r'\citet[Proposition~5.1 and formulas~(5.14)--(5.16)]{segalwilson1985loop} provides the exact result.',[SW],cls='A',reconstruction=r'Uniqueness is first asserted on the big cell; the meromorphic extension does not give a finite regular Baker value at every tau zero.')
item(r,'P001105','construction','Formal KP Lax operator and the Gelfand–Dikii reduction',r'\(Q=D+\sum q_iD^{-i}\), \(\partial_{t_r}Q=[(Q^r)_+,Q]\); when \(Q^n=L\) is differential this becomes the \(n\)th KdV hierarchy. The condition is \(z^nW\subset W\).',r'For the Baker dressing operator \(K=1+\sum a_iD^{-i}\), define \(Q=KDK^{-1}\). Then \(\partial_{t_r}Q=[(Q^r)_+,Q]\). If \((Q^n)_-=0\), the differential operator \(L=Q^n\) obeys \(\partial_{t_r}L=[(L^{r/n})_+,L]\). In the Grassmannian construction this reduction is precisely \(z^nW\subset W\). For \(L=D^2+u\), \(u=2\partial_x^2\log\tau\).',r'\citet[Propositions~5.5 and 5.9, Corollary~5.12, eq.~(5.19)]{segalwilson1985loop} is the exact source. The hierarchy formalism itself is credited there to Sato and earlier Lax theory.',[SW],cls='A',reconstruction=r'The positive part is taken after forming \(Q^r\). The factor 2 in the tau potential is required in the monic convention.',literal='false as written')
item(r,'P001105','theorem','Pole-order bounds and regular singular scalar operators',r'The notes say the resulting differential operator has only regular singular points in \(x\).',r'For a Segal--Wilson solution and fixed remaining times, the Baker coefficient \(a_i(x)\) has poles of order at most \(i\). In the reduced order-\(n\) operator \(L=D^n+\sum_{i=0}^{n-2}u_iD^i\), each coefficient has pole order at most \(n-i\) at finite poles. Thus these finite singular points are regular singular in the scalar Fuchs criterion.',r'\citet[Propositions~5.17--5.18 and their proofs in \S8]{segalwilson1985loop} proves precisely the finite-plane bounds.',[SW],cls='A',reconstruction=r'No assertion about regular singularity at infinity is added.')
item(r,'P001106','construction','The determinant line and its nontrivial central extension',r'Frames form \(\mathcal E\), trace-class changes of frame form \(\mathcal T\), and quotienting by \(\ker\det\) gives a central \(\mathbb C^*\)-extension acting on \(\mathrm{Det}\).',r'The determinant line is associated to the admissible-frame bundle by the Fredholm determinant character of \(I+\mathcal L^1\). On the identity component of the restricted linear group, pairs \((g,q)\) with \(a q^{-1}-I\) trace class form an extension; quotienting by determinant-one frame transformations gives a central \(\mathbb C^*\)-extension. It acts on the determinant line, and generally does not admit a continuous global splitting.',r'\citet[\S3, especially the discussion preceding formula~(3.5)]{segalwilson1985loop} gives the nontrivial extension and its local cocycle.',[SW],cls='A',reconstruction=r'The restricted operator ideals and the index-zero component are part of the definition; a lift is needed for the action on the line.')
item(r,'P001107','construction','Local Miura factorization and the flag of solutions',r'\(D^2+u=(D-v)(D+v)\), \(u=v_x-v^2\); the general factorization is related to analytic initial values and meromorphic flags \(SL(n)/B\).',r'Let \(L\) be monic of order \(n\) with analytic coefficients near an ordinary point and no \(D^{n-1}\) term. Complete flags in the \(n\)-dimensional local solution space determine factorizations into monic first order operators with meromorphic coefficients. Flags with all necessary initial Wronskians nonzero give analytic factors; they form an open chart with \(n(n-1)/2\) initial parameters. The second-order factorization obeys the stated Riccati relation.',r'\citet[\S3.7, Miura transformation]{drinfeldsokolov1985lie} supplies the hierarchy connection. The local flag statement follows from successive Wronskian factorization and explains the distinction drawn in the notebook.', ['drinfeldsokolov1985lie'],cls='E',reconstruction=r'This is local around an ordinary point; global meromorphic or periodic coefficients impose further monodromy constraints. The phrase every solution is not extended to global factorizations.')
item(r,'P001107 (bottom)','question','Differential fields and the proposed Galois analogy',r'The notes compare fields of coefficients, roots, and solutions, with \(B\) and \(SL(n)\) drawn beneath them, while observing that Miura fibres are not a group.',r'The Picard--Vessiot differential Galois group is a subgroup of \(SL_n\) when the \(D^{n-1}\) coefficient vanishes; a complete solution flag has a stabilizer in a Borel subgroup. Which differential field is generated by a chosen factorization depends on the operator and flag. The diagram does not specify a universal equality of Galois groups or a question to prove.',r'The Lie-theoretic Miura formalism of \citet[\S3]{drinfeldsokolov1985lie} is relevant background, but it is not an exact solution of an unstated differential-Galois problem.', ['drinfeldsokolov1985lie'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',status='As of November 2026, the analogy is meaningful but the notes do not fix one differential-field assertion.',confidence='medium')
corr('P001105','n=2 tau reconstruction',r'u=D^2\log\tau',r'u=2D^2\log\tau for L=D^2+u','Scan confirms the missing 2; Segal–Wilson eq. (5.19) and Q^2 coefficient 2q1 give the correction.')
corr('P001103','all Grassmannian points and KdV','Any W gives the stated KdV construction','Any W gives KP; nth KdV requires z^n W contained in W.','The restriction is made explicit later on P001105 and in Segal–Wilson Corollary 5.12.','ambiguity')
