# Record 594 appears before 593 in source order
r=594
talk(r,'1983-04-11','George Wilson','P001107-P001114','KP and its scalar reductions: concluding note',r'Only the lower strip of P001108 belongs to this brief conclusion. It repeats the formal KP hierarchy and its scalar differential reductions, and identifies \(2q_1\) as a KP solution. Ginzburg begins on the next page.')
item(r,'P001108','claim','The first KP coefficient and the two-time equation',r'The note repeats \(Q=D+\sum q_iD^{-i}\), its Lax equations and the reduction, then says \(2q_1(x,t_2,t_3)\) solves KP.',r'With \(x=t_1\), \(y=t_2\), \(t=t_3\) and \(u=2q_1\), the formal KP Lax equations imply \((4u_t-6uu_x-u_{xxx})_x=3u_{yy}\). The condition \(Q^n\) differential gives the Gelfand--Dikii reduction. Other signs result from explicit changes of time or leading-operator conventions.',r'This is the same formalism as 1982-592.8, proved in \citet[\S5]{segalwilson1985loop}; the short conclusion does not introduce another new theorem.',[SW],cls='E',reconstruction=r'The explicit PDE is supplied to make the coefficient assertion checkable; it is not written out on the source page.')
save()
# Record 593: actual pages extend beyond the catalogue range
r=593
talk(r,'1983-04-11','V. L. Ginzburg','P001107-P001109','Equivariant localization and the Duistermaat–Heckman theorem',r'The actual report occupies P001109--P001112, after Wilson\textquotesingle s conclusion on P001108. It compares the Duistermaat--Heckman theorem, the Atiyah--Bott equivariant interpretation, Witten\textquotesingle s parity complex, and the explicit primitive attributed to Vergne. The last page records a successful correction of an initially impossible closed angular form.')
item(r,'P001109','theorem','Exact stationary phase and the fixed-component denominator',r'For a compact symplectic manifold and Hamiltonian with compact or periodic flow, the notes write \(\int_M e^{iH}=\sum_j\operatorname{vol}(M_j)e^{iH(M_j)}/\lambda_j\), with \(\lambda_j\) a normal Pfaffian. The Fourier transform is said to have an exact stationary-phase answer.',r'Let \((M^{2n},\omega)\) be compact and let a Hamiltonian circle action have generator \(\xi\), with \(\iota_\xi\omega=dH\). For the Cartan differential \(d+u\iota_\xi\), the equivariantly closed form is \(\omega-uH\). Localization gives \[\int_M e^{\omega-uH}=\sum_{F\subset M^{S^1}}\int_F\frac{e^{\omega_F-uH_F}}{e_{S^1}(\nu F)(u)}.\] Its left side means \(\int_M e^{-uH}\omega^n/n!\), with compatible Euler-class conventions. For isolated fixed points the denominators are products of weights and powers of \(u\). For positive-dimensional fixed components the full equivariant Euler class, including curvature terms, is needed.',r'The underlying theorem was published by \citet{duistermaatheckman1982variation} in 1982. \citet[\S\S3,6--7, especially formula~(3.8)]{atiyahbott1984moment}, received 20 December 1982 and published in 1984, gives this cohomological form and distinguishes isolated points from fixed submanifolds. Its bibliography records the 1982 Berline--Vergne and Duistermaat--Heckman addendum preprints.',[AB,'duistermaatheckman1982variation'],reconstruction=r'The Liouville measure, deformation parameter, sign convention, and full fixed-component denominator are supplied. The notebook\textquotesingle s volume divided by a constant Pfaffian is not the unrestricted fixed-submanifold formula.',literal='false as written',match='partial')
item(r,'P001109','theorem','Polynomial density on regular-value chambers',r'The measure pushed forward by \(H\) is first called piecewise polynomial, then a polynomial on \(\mathbb R\). The reduced symplectic form is called linear in the level.',r'For a compact Hamiltonian torus manifold, the pushforward of Liouville measure has a polynomial density on each chamber of regular values, relative to Lebesgue measure on the moment-map image. Under the natural local identification of reduced spaces, the cohomology class of the reduced symplectic form varies affinely with the moment value. This cohomological variation gives the polynomial volume; walls can change the polynomial.',r'\citet{duistermaatheckman1982variation} is the 1982 source; \citet[\S7]{atiyahbott1984moment} gives the localization and Fourier-transform interpretation. This is not one polynomial valid across all critical values.',[AB,'duistermaatheckman1982variation'],literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'Affineness concerns the reduced cohomology class after identification, not a global choice of local coordinate expressions for every reduced two-form.')
item(r,'P001109','question','Gelfand’s question about stationary eigenvalues',r'Gelfand remarks that the eigenvalues of the stationary action have not yet been used.',r'The normal isotropy weights are precisely the linearized eigenvalue data entering the equivariant Euler denominator. No further proposed identity or missing conclusion is specified by the question.',r'\citet[\S\S3 and 7]{atiyahbott1984moment} explains where those weights occur. The question is an expository challenge to the abbreviated derivation, not a separately identifiable unresolved theorem.',[AB],cls='F',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',literal='not determinable',match='partial',confidence='medium',status='As of November 2026, the role of the normal weights is explicit in localization; no distinct open proposition is stated here.')
item(r,'P001110','construction','Borel equivariant cohomology and localization',r'\(H_G^*(M)=H^*(EG\times_GM)\), as a module over \(H^*(BG)\); for the circle \(H^*(BS^1;\mathbb Q)=\mathbb Q[u]\), \(\deg u=2\). Restriction to the fixed locus becomes an isomorphism over the fraction field. The notes recall Atiyah--Segal in K-theory and Quillen in cohomology.',r'For a compact smooth circle manifold, restriction induces \[H_{S^1}^*(M;\mathbb Q)\otimes_{\mathbb Q[u]}\mathbb Q(u)\simeq H^*(M^{S^1};\mathbb Q)\otimes\mathbb Q(u).\] More generally a torus requires localization of its polynomial coefficient ring. The analogous statement for an arbitrary nonabelian compact group cannot be obtained just by replacing the circle fixed locus by the full group fixed locus.',r'\citet[\S\S2--3]{atiyahbott1984moment} reviews the earlier Borel/Atiyah--Segal localization and proves the integration formula. The lecture\textquotesingle s later explicit restriction to \(S^1\) supplies the relevant hypothesis.',[AB],reconstruction=r'Rational coefficients and the circle or torus restriction are explicit. A polynomial description of \(H^*(BG)\) needs the corresponding coefficient and compact-Lie-group hypotheses.')
item(r,'P001111','construction','The invariant Cartan complex and the square of its differential',r'\(d_1=d+u\iota_\xi\) is first written on all forms; the notes then insist on invariant forms from the start. They print \(d_1^2=u^2L_\xi\). Goncharov compares the construction with Chern--Weil theory.',r'The Cartan model is \((\Omega(M)^{S^1}\otimes\mathbb C[u],d+u\iota_\xi)\), \(\deg u=2\). Cartan\textquotesingle s homotopy identity gives \((d+u\iota_\xi)^2=uL_\xi\), which vanishes on invariant forms. Its cohomology identifies with Borel equivariant cohomology over \(\mathbb C\); the Chern--Weil comparison uses a connection on the universal bundle.',r'\citet[\S4]{atiyahbott1984moment} gives the differential-form model and its relation with the topological construction. The invariant-form qualification is already repaired in the notebook; only the extra power of \(u\) remains an actual formula error.',[AB],literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'The later correction to invariant forms is retained as part of the source argument, rather than counted as an unresolved omission.')
item(r,'P001111-P001112','method','Parity localization and Vergne’s explicit contracting form',r'After putting \(u=1\), the notes identify the parity cohomology with that of the fixed set. They first seek an invariant angular one-form \(\alpha\) with \(\iota_\xi\alpha=1\) and \(d\alpha=0\), then replace it by \(\beta=\alpha(1+d\alpha)^{-1}\) and write \(\omega=d_1(\beta\wedge\omega)+\beta\wedge d_1\omega\).',r'On the complement of the zero set of a Killing field generating a compact action, choose an invariant metric and \(\alpha=\xi^\flat/\|\xi\|^2\). Then \(\iota_\xi\alpha=1\), \(L_\xi\alpha=0\). The finite exterior-algebra series \(\beta=\alpha\sum_{j\ge0}(-d\alpha)^j\) satisfies \((d+\iota_\xi)\beta=1\). Wedge multiplication by \(\beta\) contracts the invariant parity complex away from the fixed set. For compact \(M\), restriction therefore gives its \(\mathbb Z/2\)-graded cohomology as \(H^*(M^{S^1};\mathbb C)\) with parity grading; boundary integration near the fixed set yields the localization residues.',r'\citet[\S\S4--5 and 8]{atiyahbott1984moment} treats this model and the relation to Witten and Berline--Vergne. Its reference [5] records the Rennes Berline--Vergne preprint of 1982, before this talk. The notebook itself contains the valid primitive and the contracting identity.',[AB],reconstruction=r'\ours{} A direct check is \(D\alpha=1+d\alpha\), \(D(d\alpha)=L_\xi\alpha-d(\iota_\xi\alpha)=0\); hence \(D\beta=1\), and the graded Leibniz rule gives the written identity. This verifies the recorded proof, not a new localization theorem. The earlier aspiration \(d\alpha=0\) is explicitly abandoned.',development='annotated',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(r,'P001111','question','A group-free formulation through the double fibration',r'Gelfand asks to formulate the same theorem without \(G\), in the language of the two projections of \(EG\times_GM\).',r'The diagram alone does not specify what structures should replace the group action, the polynomial coefficient ring, the fixed set, or the localization multiplicative set. Without those data it is not a single theorem about arbitrary double fibrations.',r'The Borel and Cartan comparison in \citet[\S\S2--4]{atiyahbott1984moment} explains the diagram, but does not solve the additional unspecified group-free programme. No later groupoid or foliated theorem is treated as an exact answer.',[AB],cls='F',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',confidence='low',status='As of November 2026, the wording remains insufficient to identify one falsifiable open problem.')
corr('P001109','stationary-phase formula for fixed submanifolds','vol(Mj) exp(iH(Mj))/lambda_j','Use the full inverse equivariant Euler class on a positive-dimensional fixed component.','The normal curvature terms contribute after integration; Atiyah–Bott formula (3.8), sections 6–7 distinguish this from isolated fixed points.')
corr('P001109','pushforward and variation of symplectic form','Polynomial on R; symplectic form is linear','Polynomial on regular-value chambers; reduced cohomology class varies affinely.','The same page first says piecewise polynomial; Duistermaat–Heckman gives the chamberwise and cohomological statement.')
corr('P001111','square of the Cartan differential',r'd_1^2=u^2L_\xi',r'd_1^2=uL_\xi','Both versions and scan agree on u squared; expand (d+u i_xi)^2 using Cartan homotopy.')
corr('P001112','angular form and the subsequent repair','Initially d1 alpha=1 is sought','The successful form is beta=alpha(1+d alpha)^(-1), with d1 beta=1.','The scan explicitly gives this correction and the contracting identity; preserve the sequence, not an unresolved-error label.','ambiguity')
