# Record 596 precedes 595 on P001113
r=596
talk(r,'1983-04-18','V. L. Ginzburg','P001112-P001115','Moment-map convexity and density chambers',r'The actual report is the upper and middle portion of P001113, ending at Beilinson\textquotesingle s heading. A preceding plan mentions reading Levinson on the zeta function but supplies no theorem. P001115 starts 5 September 1983 and belongs to the next academic year, despite the catalogue overlap.')
item(r,'P001113','definition','Hamiltonian torus actions and the moment map',r'The notes start with a compact symplectic torus manifold, then insert the assignment \(X\mapsto H_X\) compatible with Poisson brackets and define \(\mu(m)(X)=H_X(m)\).',r'For a Hamiltonian action of a torus \(T\) on \((M,\omega)\), choose a linear assignment \(X\mapsto H_X\) with \(dH_X=\iota_{X_M}\omega\), compatible with the Lie and Poisson brackets. The map \(\mu:M\to\mathfrak t^*\) is defined by evaluation. A symplectic torus action need not be Hamiltonian; the inserted Hamiltonian assignment is an essential assumption.',r'\citet[\S1]{atiyah1982convexity} states the Hamiltonian setup before the convexity theorem. This continues the moment-map discussion in 1981-556.12 and 1980-508.15; it is not the exact orbital-integral question of 1982-568.', ['atiyah1982convexity'],reconstruction=r'The inserted Poisson representation is treated as part of the notes, not silently supplied by symplecticity alone.')
item(r,'P001113','theorem','Convexity of the moment image',r'\(\mu(M)\) is asserted to be a convex polytope; the margin credits Atiyah--Bott and Guillemin--Sternberg independently.',r'If a torus acts in a Hamiltonian manner on a compact connected symplectic manifold, its moment-map image is the convex hull of the finitely many images of connected fixed-point components, and its fibres are connected. Compactness and connectedness are part of the theorem.',r'\citet[Theorem~1]{atiyah1982convexity}, received 27 March 1981 and published in 1982, proves this theorem. The independent convexity work is by Guillemin--Sternberg, as also described in \citet[\S6]{atiyahbott1984moment}; the source margin conflates Atiyah\textquotesingle s convexity paper with the joint equivariant-localization work.', ['atiyah1982convexity',AB],reconstruction=r'The connected-fibre conclusion is included to identify Atiyah\textquotesingle s precise theorem, although it is not separately stated in this short note.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(r,'P001113','theorem','Critical walls and the Duistermaat–Heckman density',r'The density is piecewise polynomial on \(\mu(M)\); transitions are called faces where \(d\mu\) degenerates and are said to lie in planes.',r'The moment-map critical values lie in finitely many affine subspaces coming from isotropy strata. The regular-value chambers carry polynomial Duistermaat--Heckman densities. Critical walls may occur in the interior of the moment polytope; they are not restricted to its boundary faces.',r'This is the torus version of 1982-593.2. \citet{duistermaatheckman1982variation} and \citet[\S\S6--7]{atiyahbott1984moment} supply the precise chamber picture.', ['duistermaatheckman1982variation',AB],reconstruction=r'The word faces is read as a wall decomposition, not as an assertion that every critical value belongs to the boundary of the image.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
corr('P001113','convexity attribution','Atiyah–Bott and Guillemin–Sternberg independently','Atiyah and Guillemin–Sternberg independently for convexity; Atiyah–Bott for the equivariant localization treatment.','Atiyah1982 Theorem 1 and Atiyah–Bott1984 section 6.','faithful_error')
