# Record 595, interrupted by 598
r=595
talk(r,'1983-04-18','A. A. Beilinson','P001112-P001114','Arithmetic zeta functions and the global continuation problem',r'The talk starts in the lower portion of P001113 and continues on P001114, with a short Wodzicki interruption before Beilinson resumes. The heading promises special values up to a rational factor and exact formulations, but the recorded mathematics concerns local factors, global continuation, a Langlands-type correspondence, and the elliptic functional equation. No rank or leading-coefficient formula for Birch--Swinnerton-Dyer is actually recorded.')
BC='breuilconraddiamondtaylor2001modularity';BG='boxercalegarigeepilloni2021surfaces';BM='boxercalegarigeepilloni2025modularity';GE='gee2026modularity'
item(r,'P001113 (lower portion)','remark','Special-value programme and the limits of the surviving statement',r'The heading distinguishes formulations modulo \(\mathbb Q^*\) from exact formulations, followed by the name Birch--Swinnerton-Dyer.',r'The notes contain no regulator map, motivic cohomology group, rank equality, leading coefficient, or specified integer special value. They announce a programme rather than state a precise special-value conjecture.',r'The modern distinctions between Hasse--Weil continuation and stronger arithmetic conjectures are reviewed in \citet[\S1]{gee2026modularity}. The earlier regulator occurrence 1979-476.11 is related context, not an exact recurrence inferable from this heading.',[GE],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',confidence='high',status='As of November 2026, no particular regulator or Birch--Swinnerton-Dyer formula can be identified from this entry alone.')
item(r,'P001113 (bottom)-P001114 (upper portion)','definition','Arithmetic models and the local point-count zeta function',r'The notes choose equations supposedly giving smooth reduction at every prime, write \(\zeta(X,s)=\prod_p\zeta(X_{\mathbb F_p},s)\), and define each local factor by \(\sum_n\#X(\mathbb F_{p^n})p^{-ns}\). The point example then gives the usual Riemann Euler product.',r'For a smooth projective geometrically connected curve \(X/\mathbb Q\), there is a smooth proper model over \(\mathbb Z[1/S]\) for a finite set of bad primes \(S\); changing equations cannot generally remove bad reduction. At a good prime, \[Z(X_p,t)=\exp\!\left(\sum_{n\ge1}\#X_p(\mathbb F_{p^n})\frac{t^n}{n}\right),\qquad t=p^{-s}.\] Bad local factors require the chosen arithmetic zeta or cohomological L-function convention. A point has \(Z(t)=(1-t)^{-1}\); the unexponentiated series is the logarithmic derivative data, not the local zeta function.',r'\citet[\S1]{deligne1974weil} gives the finite-field zeta and cohomological factors. \citet[\S1.1]{boxercalegarigeepilloni2021surfaces} explicitly separates the global Euler product and finitely many excluded factors.', ['deligne1974weil',BG],literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'The finite bad set, exponential, and divisor \(n\) are indispensable. The word rational in the margin is not interpreted as a genus-zero hypothesis because the continuation discussion explicitly addresses general curves.')
item(r,'P001114 (upper portion)','theorem','Weil rationality and the correct location of finite-field zeros',r'The local function is rational in \(p^{-s}\), with numerator and denominator factors; a damaged formula places zeros at \(\operatorname{Re}z=p^{?/2}\). The notes remark that the curve case is proved.',r'For a smooth projective geometrically connected curve of genus \(g\) over \(\mathbb F_p\), \[Z(X,t)=\frac{\prod_{j=1}^{2g}(1-\alpha_jt)}{(1-t)(1-pt)},\qquad |\alpha_j|=p^{1/2}.\] Thus the numerator zeros in the \(t\)-plane have modulus \(p^{-1/2}\); in the variable \(t=p^{-s}\), they lie on \(\Re s=1/2\). These statements concern a local finite-field factor, not the global Riemann hypothesis.',r'\citet[Theorem~1.6]{deligne1974weil} proves purity in every cohomological degree for smooth projective varieties, not merely curves. The paper records July 1973 lectures and was published in 1974; both precede this 1983 exposition.', ['deligne1974weil'],literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'The damaged exponent is not guessed. The three distinct variables---Frobenius eigenvalue, local zero, and complex s---are separated.')
item(r,'P001114','question','Which coefficients define reasonable Euler factors?',r'Gelfand asks which \(a_n(p)\) in \(\prod_p\sum_n a_n(p)p^{-ns}\) should be called reasonable, observing that \(a_n(p)=1\) for the Riemann zeta function.',r'The question does not impose local rationality, integrality, purity, conductor bounds, compatibility across primes, or an automorphic or motivic origin. These choices give inequivalent classification problems.',r'\citet[\S1]{gee2026modularity} specifies the geometric and Galois data in a modern Hasse--Weil question. It does not supply a necessary-and-sufficient characterization of the undefined word reasonable.',[GE],cls='F',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',confidence='medium',status='As of November 2026, the wording does not identify one precise open classification problem.')
item(r,'P001114','problem','Global continuation and functional equations for curves',r'The notes say analytic continuation is not proved even for curves over \(\mathbb Q\), contrast the finite-field case, and locate the obstacle in compatibility among different primes.',r'For every smooth projective geometrically connected curve over \(\mathbb Q\), prove meromorphic continuation of its completed arithmetic zeta function and the expected functional equation, with the local bad factors and archimedean factors specified. Equivalently, one may formulate continuation for the Hasse--Weil L-function of its \(H^1\), after separating the Tate factors. This is a global assertion; finite-field rationality alone does not imply it.',r'For genus one over \(\mathbb Q\), \citet[Theorem~A]{breuilconraddiamondtaylor2001modularity} proves modularity and hence the analytic assertion (2001). For every genus-two curve over a totally real field, \citet[Theorem~1.1.2]{boxercalegarigeepilloni2021surfaces} proves meromorphic continuation and the functional equation, by potential automorphy in Theorem~1.1.3. Their work circulated at the end of 2018, was received 25 December 2018, and appeared in 2021. \citet[\S1 and Remark~2.7]{gee2026modularity} gives the November 2026 research perspective.',[BC,BG,GE],cls='C',problem='GP-1982-42',worth='Worth pursuing in explicit higher-genus families because general global continuation remains unresolved and local point-count data alone do not control the Euler product.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',status=r'As of November 2026, the unrestricted curve statement remains open. Genus one over \(\mathbb Q\) and genus two over totally real fields have the stated continuation results. Potential automorphy gives meromorphic continuation over the original field; it does not establish holomorphy of every genus-two \(H^1\) L-function.',reconstruction=r'The precise completion and the distinction between the arithmetic zeta function and its \(H^1\) L-factor are supplied. The historical sentence is read as a general conjecture, not as denying all previously known special curves.')
item(r,'P001114','conjecture','Matching geometric H1 factors with automorphic L-functions',r'A strong conjecture says the two classes of geometric zeta functions and Langlands zeta functions coincide.',r'A precise direction relevant to a curve is that the compatible Galois representations on its \(H^1\) should correspond to suitable algebraic automorphic data, allowing isobaric decompositions when the motive is reducible, with matching good Euler factors and compatible completions. Literal equality with every automorphic L-function is too broad: algebraicity, weight, and motivic constraints matter.',r'\citet[Theorem~1.1.3]{boxercalegarigeepilloni2021surfaces} gives potential automorphy for all genus-two curves over totally real fields. Stronger actual modularity is proved under explicit extra hypotheses by \citet[Theorem~B]{boxercalegarigeepilloni2025modularity}: for a genus-two curve \(y^2=f(x)\) over \(\mathbb Q\), \(\deg f=5\), surjective mod 3 representation on its Jacobian and good ordinary reduction at 2 and 3 imply modularity. The preprint was posted 28 February 2025. \citet[Remark~2.7]{gee2026modularity} places this advance in the continuing programme.',[BG,BM,GE],cls='C',problem='GP-1982-43',worth='Worth developing for carefully specified motives or explicit curves because actual automorphy is stronger than potential automorphy and can yield holomorphy as well as continuation.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',status=r'As of November 2026, general actual automorphy for arbitrary curve \(H^1\) remains open. The 2025 theorem has the stated genus-two, rational-Weierstrass-point, residual-image and ordinary-reduction hypotheses; it is not modularity of every abelian surface.',reconstruction=r'The formulation is one modern geometric-to-automorphic direction extracted from the broad notebook sentence, not a claim that the notes formulated a complete Langlands reciprocity conjecture.')
item(r,'P001114 (bottom, after Wodzicki)','claim','The elliptic completion and what the BSD heading does not say',r'Under the resumed Birch--Swinnerton-Dyer heading, only continuation and symmetry of \((2\pi)^{-s}\Gamma(s)\zeta(X,s)\) under \(s\mapsto2-s\) are written; the strip \(0\le\Re s\le2\) is drawn.',r'For an elliptic curve \(E/\mathbb Q\) with conductor \(N\), the completed Hasse--Weil L-function is \(\Lambda(E,s)=N^{s/2}(2\pi)^{-s}\Gamma(s)L(E,s)\), and \(\Lambda(E,s)=w_E\Lambda(E,2-s)\), where \(w_E\in\{\pm1\}\). The whole arithmetic zeta function of the curve contains additional Tate factors and has a different completion. Neither the equality of analytic and algebraic ranks nor the BSD leading-term formula appears in these notes.',r'\citet[Theorem~A]{breuilconraddiamondtaylor2001modularity} establishes modularity of every elliptic curve over \(\mathbb Q\), hence the continuation and functional equation written here after these corrections. The broader BSD conjecture is not thereby solved and is not manufactured as a separate occurrence.',[BC],cls='B',literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'The conductor, root number, and identification of the displayed series with \(L(H^1(E),s)\) are necessary emendations; genus one is inferred from the single gamma factor and the named conjecture, with that inference made explicit.')
