item(r,'P001114 (middle interruption)','method','Dedekind zeta and Shintani cone series',r'Wodzicki asks which zeta functions can be computed and cites Shintani, writing \(\zeta_K(s)=\sum_{\mathfrak a}N\mathfrak a^{-s}=\sum_n a_n n^{-s}\).',r'For a number field \(K\), the sum is over nonzero integral ideals, \(N\mathfrak a=|\mathcal O_K/\mathfrak a|\), and converges for \(\Re s>1\). For totally real \(K\), Shintani\textquotesingle s cone decomposition expresses partial Dedekind zeta functions as finite sums of shifted positive-cone Dirichlet series associated with products of real linear forms; these yield values at nonpositive integers. The totally real assumption belongs to this particular construction.',r'\citet[opening pages and \S1]{cassounogues1982series} explicitly states Shintani\textquotesingle s 1976 result and develops the associated polynomial Dirichlet series. It was a 1981 arithmetic-days report, published in 1982, before this talk. The original 1976 paper\textquotesingle s bibliographic identity is confirmed by that primary research account; its full text was not obtained here.', ['cassounogues1982series'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The nonzero-integral-ideal condition and totally real scope are supplied; the notes do not specify which special values or which field signature.')
item(r,'P001114 (middle interruption)','claim','Constant-coefficient operators on a torus: unresolved identification',r'The Shintani construction is described as reducing the Dedekind zeta function to zeta functions of pseudodifferential operators on a torus with constant coefficients.',r'A cone-restricted polynomial lattice sum can be viewed formally as a spectral sum after choosing a Fourier multiplier and a projection onto a cone of modes. That does not by itself make it the zeta function of an ordinary positive classical elliptic operator on the whole torus. The symbol, ellipticity, multiplicities, cone projection and zero-mode convention must be specified.',r'The primary account \citet{cassounogues1982series} verifies the cone Dirichlet series but does not identify the unrestricted torus pseudodifferential assertion written here. Searches combining Shintani, Dedekind zeta, torus, constant coefficients and pseudodifferential operators did not locate an exact operator theorem. This is an unresolved identification, not evidence that the arithmetic method was undeveloped.', ['cassounogues1982series'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',status='As of November 2026, the Shintani arithmetic construction is established; the exact operator realization intended by the note has not been identified.',reconstruction=r'The Fourier-multiplier interpretation is supplied only to explain what additional data an exact statement would require, and is not claimed as a recovered historical theorem.')
